<p>This is a Critiquing Forum: I'm inclined to help.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>My initial thought is that the concept is very relatable, but you wont get much traction.</p>
<p>Your pitch is short and sweet; no arguments, not much to say. Then we reach the story, and I'm afraid I've got some bad-news, friend.</p>
<p>People on this site are all-about the stories: if your object is intended to simply BE an object, an anomaly about which people are expected to draw their own conclusions and think for themselves, it is unlikely to be well-received, so I've been told. Try to come up with some meta-narrative for where the key-chain came-from, why it does what it does, and what it will do in the future.</p>
<p>The Hook doesn't seem especially 'baited', I advise you to look more deeply into what will draw the reader into this SCP concept.</p>
<p>All-in-all: I can understand why you're not looking for a green-light yet: I encourage you to keep working.</p>
</div>
